          "path": "_m/f/8/d/k/perce-rock-wp/getting-to-perce-rock.jpg",
          "filename": "Getting to Perce Rock.jpg",
          "caption": "Walking over rugged shale to get to the sandbar to Perce Rock is only half the battle!  Name by Samuel de Champlain in 1607, Perce Rock is a massive limestone and shale formation of 433 metres long, 90 metres wide and 88 metres high with an arch at one end 15 metres high. Situated in the town of Perce Quebec, it can be accessed by foot at low tide by a sandbar to its southwest. The rock has over 150 species of fossils. It is also a bird colony to many species such as snowy gannets, silvery gulls, black cormorants etc.",
          "alt": "Walking over rugged shale to get to the sandbar to Perce Rock is only half the battle!  Name by Samuel de Champlain in 1607, Perce Rock is a massive limestone and shale formation of 433 metres long, 90 metres wide and 88 metres high with an arch at one end 15 metres high. Situated in the town of Perce Quebec, it can be accessed by foot at low tide by a sandbar to its southwest. The rock has over 150 species of fossils. It is also a bird colony to many species such as snowy gannets, silvery gulls, black cormorants etc.",
